{
  "question": "In the context of Grothendieck's theory of schemes, which specific property of the stalks of the structure sheaf $\mathcal{O}_X$ is required for a locally ringed space to be classified as a scheme, thereby enabling the rigorous handling of singularities?",
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "A",
      "text": "The stalks must be integral domains."
    },
    {
      "id": "B",
      "text": "The stalks must be global fields."
    },
    {
      "id": "C",
      "text": "The stalks must be local rings."
    },
    {
      "id": "D",
      "text": "The stalks must be Noetherian rings."
    }
  ],
  "correct_option": "C",
  "explanation": "A scheme is defined as a locally ringed space $(X, \mathcal{O}_X)$ such that every point $x \in X$ has an open neighborhood isomorphic to the affine scheme $\text{Spec}(A)$ for some commutative ring $A$. The fundamental requirement of a 'locally ringed' space is that the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ at every point must be a **local ring**. This allows for the presence of nilpotent elements and complex singularities that are excluded if stalks were required to be integral domains or fields. Condition (D) is not strictly necessary for the general definition of a scheme, although Noetherian schemes are often studied in relative contexts."
}

