    "A": "It decreases the shear force on the knee, requiring significantly less eccentric quadriceps activity to stabilize the joint.",
    "B": "It increases the anterior shear force across the knee joint, potentially elevating the risk of lateral collaterals or tibial translation injuries.",
    "C": "It shifts the center of pressure posteriorly, enhancing not only ankle dorsiflexor torque but also reducing the vertical ground reaction force.",
    "D": "It reduces the hip flexion moment, allowing the athlete to maintain a longer flight phase to improve velocity without impacting vertical impulse."
  },
