  "explanation": "During the eccentric phase of landing, the direction and magnitude of the ground reaction force (GRF) vector determine the joint moments and shear forces across the knee. An anterior GRF vector position (relative to the knee joint center) creates a moment arm that tends to translate the tibia anteriorly relative to the femur (anterior shear force). This mechanical environment is a known risk factor for ACL sprains and meniscal injuries. While quadriceps activation is required to produce a counter-moment to stabilize the knee, the physics of the vector summation directly dictates the shear load. Options A, C, and D misrepresent the kinematic and kinetic relationships between GRF vector position, joint moments, and ankle or hip mechanics."
}

