{
  "question": "Consider the Cartesian product $X = \\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ of a family of compact topological spaces $\{X_i\}_{i \\in I}$ indexed by an arbitrary set $I$. The Tychonoff Theorem asserts that $X$ is compact under the standard product topology. However, when equipped with the box topology, the behavior of compactness changes significantly. Which of the following assertions regarding the compactness of $X$ in the box topology is mathematically correct (assuming the spaces $X_i$ are non-trivial)?",
  "options": {
    "A": "The space $X$ is compact in the box topology for any index set $I$, provided the spaces $X_i$ are Hausdorff.",
    "B": "The space $X$ is compact in the box topology if and only if the index set $I$ is finite.",
    "C": "The space $X$ is compact in the box topology provided $I$ is countable and the spaces $X_i$ are compact metric spaces.",
    "D": "The space $X$ is compact in the box topology for any uncountable index set $I$ if the factors are intervals in $\\mathbb{R}$."
  },
  "correct_option": "B",
  "explanation": "The Tychonoff Theorem guarantees compactness in the product topology for arbitrary products of compact spaces, relying on the finite union property of open covers. The box topology has a strictly finer basis than the product topology, meaning it contains more open sets. Consequently, it is strictly harder to find a finite subcover for a given open cover in the box topology. A classical result in topology states that the infinite product of non-trivial compact spaces is never compact in the box topology. Therefore, compactness holds for any index set $I$ if and only if $I$ is finite. Options A, C, and D claim compactness holds for infinite $I$ under specific conditions, which is false. Specifically, for $I$ infinite, one can construct an open cover in the box topology that has no finite subcover (e.g., using $\\{0, 1\\}^I$ or unit intervals)."
}

